  "title": "Left-hand octaves",
  "summary": "Hold an octave shape and move it, like the left hand of Moonlight Sonata.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "The octave shape",
      "body": "The left hand of Moonlight Sonata plays two keys with the same name at once: a low one with the little finger (5) and the one eight white keys up with the thumb (1).\n\nThat stretched hand is the **octave shape**: thumb and little finger an octave apart, the middle fingers floating, not pressing.\n\n**Why it matters:** keep the shape and move the whole hand as one, and every octave lands without looking. Squeezing the hand to find each key again is what makes octaves slow.\n\nThe real piece is lower than your keyboard may go, so this session plays the same moves higher up, in C minor. Press **Watch**.",
